5, -0.02960205078125, -0.0031986236572265625, 0.0015401840209960938, 0.02227783203125, -0.03240966796875, -0.0135040283203125, 0.01351165771484375, -0.0175018310546875, 0.00482940673828125, -0.005893707275390625, 0.0111541748046875, 0.05364990234375, 0.03167724609375, 0.0017232894897460938, -0.0017480850219726562, -0.0151824951171875, -0.0005679130554199219, 0.033935546875, -0.036376953125, -0.0112762451171875, -0.037628173828125, -0.0292205810546875, 0.0247039794921875, -0.012908935546875, 0.00881195068359375, -0.031280517578125, 0.018951416015625, 0.013824462890625, 0.004589080810546875, -0.002307891845703125, -0.0035152435302734375, -0.0139312744140625, 0.00557708740234375, -0.01467132568359375, 0.01111602783203125, -0.0116729736328125, 0.0205841064453125, 0.00940704345703125, 0.0065460205078125, -0.00443267822265625, 0.01345062255859375, 0.0294036865234375, -0.006038665771484375, 0.0364990234375, -0.020294189453125, -0.0116119384765625, 0.01335906982421875, -0.0125579833984375, -0.00847625732421875, 0.0023956298828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. However, this task is rendered complex by the diversity in organizational accounting practices\u2014a phenomenon we describe as \u201cunique accounting contexts.\u201d In our study, unique accounting contexts were systematically identified through an initial audit of each organization's chart of accounts, accounting policy documents, and prior GL-coded transaction logs. Criteria for delineation included: (a) custom vendor-to-GL code mappings, (b) presence of custom, nested GL hierarchies, (c) use of nonstandard jargon or acronyms in invoice descriptions, and (d) deviation from accounting industry default categories. For example, while one company categorized software subscriptions as \u201cIT Services,\u201d another used \u201cProfessional Fees\u201d; similarly, invoices from the same vendor could be mapped to different GL codes depending on client-specific usage. These examples illustrate the concrete, policy-driven drivers of diversity across organizations.\n\nThis diversity extended to how organizations grouped expenses: some categorized all employee expenses broadly, while others used highly specific GL codes. To capture this, we systematically collected metadata for each client\u2019s chart of accounts and codified explicit GL code assignment rules. These business rules\u2014such as precedence for vendor-specific codes or context-dependent assignment by keyword\u2014were encoded as structured features in our dataset.\n\n**Methods**\n\nWe evaluated whether small in-house language models (SLMs)\u2014specifically, SBERT (Sentence-BERT, a sentence transformer) and DeBERTa (a classic small transformer-based language model)\u2014can compete with larger, cloud-based models with respect to accuracy, data security, and interpretability. SLMs here are defined as transformer models small enough for on-premises deployment and feasible client-specific fine-tuning. We selected SBERT due to its established semantic representation capabilities for sentence-level tasks, and DeBERTa for its robust contextual encoding in low-resource settings. Hyperparameters (learning rates, optimizer, batch size, epochs) were chosen following recommendations from prior invoice classification literature [1,2] and tuned via 5-fold cross-validation, comparing grid searches for learning rate (1e-6 to 2e-5), batch sizes (16, 32), and patience values (2\u20135). Chosen values balanced validation F1 stability and computational efficiency. Model configurations are detailed in Supplementary Methods Table 1.\n\nOur dataset comprised 10,000 labeled invoices from mid-sized manufacturing, consulting, and logistics firms, covering 45 GL codes. GL code frequencies were highly skewed: the most common label appeared on 3,000 invoices, while the rarest appeared on 30; the top 10 codes comprised 60% of all labels. The data spanned 300+ vendors and variable invoice templates. For preprocessing, invoices were split by structured fields (vendor, date, line items, total, description) and raw OCR text. Standard steps included lower-casing, tokenization, removal of duplicates, and normalization of numerical fields to strings. For ambiguous or low-confidence field extractions (<0.4 extractor confidence or <5% field occurrence), fields were set to \u201c[BLANK]\u201d (n=422, 4.2% of all entries) or excluded from feature construction. Approximately 7% of invoices contained at least one imputed or omitted field. To mitigate bias, we confirmed via error analysis that invoices with missing fields had similar code distributions to the overall sample (see Supplementary Table 3).\n\nAnnotation followed a two-stage process: professional accountants first fine-tuned draft labels generated by accounting software using comprehensive instructions, emphasizing correct code assignments per each client\u2019s policy and explicit handling of ambiguous/multi-category items (instruction set in Supplementary Protocol 1). Ambiguous cases (723 invoices, 7.2%) were independently labeled by two annotators; persistent disagreements (34 cases) were resolved by consensus during adjudication meetings moderated by a senior accountant who referenced company-specific policies. Agreement, as measured by Cohen\u2019s kappa, averaged 0.83 on a 500-sample subset after consensus steps.\n\nThe dataset was de-identified in compliance with data privacy requirements. We held out 10% of invoices (1,000), stratified across GL codes and vendors, for final evaluation. Hyperparameter optimization (learning rate, batch size) was driven by maximizing mean cross-validation F1 while maintaining training stability; the final configuration (SBERT: 2e-5 lr, DeBERTa: 1e-5, batch size 32, patience 3) reflected this balance.\n\nBaselines comprised: (a) a zero-shot large language model (OpenAI GPT-3 via API) prompted as \u201cAssign the following invoice text to the most appropriate GL code from this list: [GL codes]. Invoice: [invoice text].\u201d We tested five prompt variants; results varied by \u22640.02 F1. (b) a vendor history rule, assigning the most frequent GL code per vendor from training data.\n\n**Results**\n\nTable 1 presents core results for the 1,000-invoice test set. SBERT achieved 0.96 accuracy and 0.93 mean micro F1; DeBERTa reached 0.93 accuracy and 0.89 F1. The zero-shot LLM baseline performed at 0.83 accuracy/0.81 F1, and the vendor-based rule achieved 0.74/0.68. Macro-F1 values were 0.84 (SBERT), 0.81 (DeBERTa), 0.69 (LLM), 0.49 (vendor). For rare GL codes (<1% prevalence), SBERT\u2019s recall averaged 0.39 and precision 0.36, against 0.16/0.13 for the LLM. For the 10 most/least frequent codes, Table 2 details per-class precision, recall, and F1 (full confusion matrix and further per-class calibration plots are in Supplementary Tables 2 and 4). The lowest per-class F1 for SBERT was 0.37, coinciding with the smallest cluster sizes and highest label ambiguity.\n\nTo check robustness, we computed 95% confidence intervals using 1,000 stratified bootstrap samples. E.g., SBERT accuracy CI: [0.957, 0.965]; differences between SLMs and baselines were statistically significant (p<0.01). An error analysis (Table 3) identified typical confusions: overlapping codes for \u201coffice supplies\u201d vs. \u201cadmin overhead,\u201d and misclassifications for unseen vendors. Calibration curves (Supplementary Fig. 1) revealed mild overconfidence for rare codes; overall, SLMs demonstrated moderate calibration (Brier score 0.13 for SBERT).\n\nSLMs transferred to new clients efficiently: SBERT achieved 0.90 F1 after training on ~100 client invoices. Rare GL codes, though challenging, still saw SLM F1 advantage over baselines of \u22650.15.\n\nClass imbalance was addressed by stratified sampling and weighted loss functions. Oversampling rare classes provided \u22640.01 F1 gain but poorer validation stability, reinforcing our design choice. Overall, no material performance gain was seen using oversampling.\n\n**Interpretability**\n\nModel interpretability is a core requirement. Qualitatively, we visualized SBERT and DeBERTa attention maps, finding consistent emphasis on line item descriptions and vendor names in high-confidence cases; diffuse or inconsistent attention strongly associated with low-confidence or incorrect predictions. Quantitatively, we used integrated gradients to rank input feature importance, confirming line description, vendor, and (if present) item price contributed most to model output (see feature importance plots in Supplementary Fig. 2). For counterfactual analysis, altering keywords (e.g., replacing \u201crent\u201d with \u201csubscription\u201d in descriptions) led to systematic GL code changes, supporting the models' reliance on semantic clues.\n\nEmbedding space analysis showed global anisotropy, with invoices clustering tightly by frequent vendor-code pairs. Mean silhouette score was 0.52; intra-class distance for common codes (0.39\u00b10.05) was lower than for rare codes (0.58\u00b10.09). Notably, frequent vendors had higher per-class F1 and more compact clusters; these findings clarify the connection between representation quality and classification accuracy.\n\n**Comparisons to Prior Work**\n\nIn Table 4, we directly compare our SLMs to prior results. SBERT outperforms the best result in [1] (SBERT: 0.93 F1 vs. 0.89 for prior domain-tuned transformer) on a more vendor-diverse dataset. Sun et al. [2] reported F1 of 0.82\u20130.91 for custom NLP pipelines on structured invoices; our method matches or exceeds these scores, even with unstructured OCR-based data. Importantly, contrary to [4], we found no significant SLM benefit using structured fields over raw text (\u0394F1 <0.01); this supports streamlined, less labor-intensive deployment (see comparative chart in Supplementary Fig. 3).\n\n**Discussion and Limitations**\n\nOur work demonstrates that small, fine-tuned SLMs deliver high-accuracy, interpretable, and private solutions for invoice categorization, robustly outperforming LLMs and rule-based baselines. Methodological contributions include explicit vendor/business rule encoding, transparent annotation protocols, and systematic embedding/feature importance analyses. Key model and error analyses are summarized in Tables 1\u20134 and Figures 1\u20132.\n\nLimitations remain. Our study is constrained to three sectors and 10,000 invoices, leaving open the generalizability to sectors such as retail, healthcare, or government, and highly atypical invoice formats. Approximately 7% of invoices contained imputed or excluded metadata, potentially biasing results\u2014though limited error analysis suggested minimal impact on GL code distribution. Annotation, while consensus-driven, is still subject to residual label noise from ambiguous cases or incomplete vendor data. Model performance on extremely rare GL codes (<"}}